  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure has strengthened after the weak trough passed\nthrough last Wed afternoon/evening. A very unstable air mass\nover eastern Idaho along with good amounts of moisture as\nevidenced by the dewpoints above 50 deg F. This should trigger a\npenultimate round of thunderstorms for the remainder of the \nafternoon and into the evening tonight. However, a shift in the\nupper level wind to a more westerly direction will cut off the\nsupply of this unstable moist air and by Sat the risk of any\nprecipitation anywhere in the forecast area drops to zero and\nstays that way through at least next Thu. \n\nLack of clouds will allow hit temperatures to return by Sat,\nwith forecasts of at least the upper 90s for elevations below\n5500ft and some places exceeding 100 deg F on Sat. There is a\nslight cool down on Sun as a dry cold front centered in western Canada\nmoves through eastern Idaho longitudes. The main effect will be\nto increase the wind for Sat and Sun. This should mean critical\nfire weather issues discussed below.\n\nAfter the Canadian front departs the west, the upper level flow\ncontinues out of the west and northwest. This means much drier\nair and an end to thunderstorm activity. The cooling trend also\ncontinues into Mon afternoon as even Snake River plain\ntemperatures only warm into the 80s. The west and northwest flow\nslows the warming, but by Wed temperatures will be back above\n90 for the lower elevations. High pressure will keep wind light\nto with moderate gusts in the afternoon possible.",
